        "math_academy_id": "q-279506",
        "problem": "Consider the matrix $E=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ If $f(X)={X}^{2}+4X-5$, find $f(E).$\n\n| $f(E)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |",
        "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+4X-5 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+4X-5I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(E)={E}^{2}+4E-5I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${E}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{2} & =E\\cdot E \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(E):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(E) & ={E}^{2}+4E-5I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4+8-5 & 0+0-0 \\\\ 3+4-0 & 1+4-5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
